    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-calculus-1999-p5.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Assume that the continued fraction converges (it does) so that \\(f(x)\\) is well defined. Notice that \\(f(x)-x=\\frac{1}{x+f(x)}\\), so \\(f(x)^{2}-x^{2}=1\\), or \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{1+x^{2}}\\) (we need the positive square root since \\(x>0\\) ). Thus \\(f^{\\prime}(x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+x^{2}}}\\), so \\(f(x) f^{\\prime}(x)=x\\). In particular, \\(f(99) f^{\\prime}(99)=\\mathbf{9 9}\\).",
